@anon.6e01a69b2c234dfdc5e23dbc943ea0bc.1377 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1147 x i8] }> <{ [1147 x i8] c"A map from string keys to values.\0A\0AYou can construct a dictionary by enclosing comma-separated `key: value`\0Apairs in parentheses. The values do not have to be of the same type. Since\0Aempty parentheses already yield an empty array, you have to use the special\0A`(:)` syntax to create an empty dictionary.\0A\0AA dictionary is conceptually similar to an array, but it is indexed by\0Astrings instead of integers. You can access and create dictionary entries\0Awith the `.at()` method. If you know the key statically, you can\0Aalternatively use [field access notation]($scripting/#fields) (`.key`) to\0Aaccess the value. Dictionaries can be added with the `+` operator and\0A[joined together]($scripting/#blocks). To check whether a key is present in\0Athe dictionary, use the `in` keyword.\0A\0AYou can iterate over the pairs in a dictionary using a [for\0Aloop]($scripting/#loops). This will iterate in the order the pairs were\0Ainserted / declared.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let dict = (\0A  name: \22Typst\22,\0A  born: 2019,\0A)\0A\0A#dict.name \\\0A#(dict.launch = 20)\0A#dict.len() \\\0A#dict.keys() \\\0A#dict.values() \\\0A#dict.at(\22born\22) \\\0A#dict.insert(\22city\22, \22Berlin \22)\0A#(\22name\22 in dict)\0A```" }>, align 1
